  "publishedAt": "2026-04-20T15:38:11-06:00",
  "body": "\u003cp\u003eLast year, my daughter and I (and some other family and friends) did a lot of\ncamping. It was something that was difficult to do while my wife was sick, so it\nfelt like it was a long time coming.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDuring the beginning of my\n\u003ca href=\"https://synic.dev/article/2026-02-11/2025-my-year-of-loss-grief-burnout-and-self-reflection\"\u003eburnout\u003c/a\u003e,\nI started getting this itch to get out somewhere truly alone — somewhere nobody\ncould reach me and I could just sit with my own thoughts for a while.\nI decided I was going to go on a solo backpacking trip.\nThe problem was, it was November, and most of our mountain areas were already\nburied in snow. I also wanted somewhere that would be, in my mind, “solo\nbackpacking training wheels” — so I picked\n\u003ca href=\"https://stateparks.utah.gov/parks/antelope-island/\"\u003eAntelope Island\u003c/a\u003e, a little\nisland sitting on the East side of the Great Salt Lake.\nIt has some mountainous terrain, but it’s low enough that snow wasn’t an issue\nyet.\nIt’s close to civilization, cell service is spotty but present, and the most\ndangerous thing out there is supposed to be the bison.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI ended up being spectacularly wrong about the “training wheels” part.\nIt was an incredible trip and exactly what I needed, but there were a couple of\nmoments where I genuinely considered hitting the emergency button on my Garmin.\nAs you can see below, I had some unexpected visitors …\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpicture\u003e\u003csource type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-by-tent-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-by-tent-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-by-tent-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-by-tent.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003cimg class=\"photo lightbox-img\" src=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-by-tent.webp\" alt=\"Bison by my tent\" loading=\"eager\" decoding=\"sync\" fetchpriority=\"high\" width=\"1600\" height=\"1067\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-by-tent-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-by-tent-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-by-tent-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-by-tent.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003c/picture\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAntelope Island has a few camping spots, most of them right off the road.\nBut they also have 4 backcountry sites tucked away with a roughly 5 mile\napproach that you have to reserve ahead of time.\nI searched everywhere for a map showing exactly where the sites were, but the\nbest I could find was a vague circle on the island’s trail map.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI found the trailhead, strapped on my 32lb pack (you have to pack in all your\nwater — there’s no potable source on the island), and set off.\nAbout a mile in, I spotted another backpacker heading the opposite direction,\nback toward the parking lot.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e“Hey, you coming from the backcountry sites?”\nI called out. “Do you know where they actually are?”\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHe shook his head. “I have no idea where they are either.\nI was going to camp tonight, but there’s a herd of bison crossing the trail up\nahead and I’ve been waiting for like an hour.\nI’m done.”\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSure enough, I could see them in the distance — a massive herd of bison, slowly\ndrifting across the trail like they had nowhere to be.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpicture\u003e\u003csource type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-crossing-trail-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-crossing-trail-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-crossing-trail-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-crossing-trail.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003cimg class=\"photo lightbox-img\" src=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-crossing-trail.webp\" alt=\"Bison crossing the trail\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1600\" height=\"1067\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-crossing-trail-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-crossing-trail-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-crossing-trail-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-crossing-trail.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003c/picture\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBy the time I reached them, it was the tail end of the crossing and I only had\nto wait about half an hour.\nLooking back, if I had known the trail better and where the backcountry sites\nwere relative to my position, I might have realized where that herd was heading\n…\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAlong the way I passed a few more bison, some elk, and a surprising number of\nmountain bikers. When I finally rounded the massive rock formation to my left\n(called Elephant Rock), the landscape opened up and I could see the mouth of\nSplit Rock Canyon — where the backcountry campsites are tucked away.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpicture\u003e\u003csource type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/split-rock-canyon-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/split-rock-canyon-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/split-rock-canyon-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/split-rock-canyon.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003cimg class=\"photo lightbox-img\" src=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/split-rock-canyon.webp\" alt=\"Split Rock Canyon\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1600\" height=\"1067\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/split-rock-canyon-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/split-rock-canyon-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/split-rock-canyon-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/split-rock-canyon.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003c/picture\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI found the fork in the trail that led to the campsite and started setting up.\nWhile I was inflating my sleeping pad, I heard grunting in the distance.\nLow, rumbling sounds echoing off the canyon walls.\nI looked up and saw the bison herd — the same one from earlier — approaching\nfrom a completely different direction, heading straight for the canyon I was\ncamping in. They were still pretty far away, but they were moving with purpose.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHere’s my crudely marked up map.\nThe blue line is the trail I hiked in on.\nThe green line is the path the bison herd took.\nIt looks like they walked straight into the lake, but unfortunately, the Great\nSalt Lake has receded so much that their path is now solid ground.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpicture\u003e\u003csource type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/island-map-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/island-map-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/island-map-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/island-map.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003cimg class=\"photo lightbox-img\" src=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/island-map.webp\" alt=\"Map of my route and the bison herd\u0026#39;s route\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1600\" height=\"1221\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/island-map-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/island-map-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/island-map-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/island-map.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003c/picture\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBy the time I finished with my sleeping pad, the herd was almost on top of me.\nI had assumed they’d keep going in a straight line and pass right by camp, but I\nwas wrong — they turned, and it happened way faster than I expected.\nI grabbed a few essentials, zipped up the tent, and scrambled toward the\nhillside where I could duck behind some rocks if things got dicey.\nThe park website is very clear: do not approach the bison, \u003cem\u003eespecially\u003c/em\u003e if there\nare calves. Well, there were calves.\nI wasn’t taking any chances.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOne problem, though.\nIn my rush, I left all my water, my warm layers, and — most critically — my\nGarmin in the tent. All I had was my phone, which thankfully had just enough\nsignal to make a call if I really needed to.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSo I sat there and waited.\nI had bolted for the hills around 4:00 in the afternoon.\nThe herd didn’t fully clear the campsite until about 5:30. The sun was going\ndown, the temperature was dropping, and I was sitting on a rock in a t-shirt\nwatching bison graze next to my tent.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOnce they finally settled into the canyon beyond camp, I crept back, made\ndinner, ate, took some photos, and crawled into my sleeping bag.\nI could hear the bison all night — grunting, shuffling, occasionally snorting.\nIt was impossible to tell if they were right outside my tent or a mile away.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen I got up in the morning, sure enough, they were still hanging around in the\ncanyon, but slowly drifting south, away from me.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpicture\u003e\u003csource type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/morning-canyon-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/morning-canyon-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/morning-canyon-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/morning-canyon.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003cimg class=\"photo lightbox-img\" src=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/morning-canyon.webp\" alt=\"Bison in the canyon in the morning\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1600\" height=\"1067\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/morning-canyon-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/morning-canyon-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/morning-canyon-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/morning-canyon.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003c/picture\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe day before, while I was setting up camp, I had noticed a large cliff behind\nme. I could hear voices carrying down from the top — when I looked up, I saw two\npeople sitting on the edge, just taking in the view.\nOn my way out the next morning, I decided to take a detour and check it out for\nmyself.\nIt was a 2.5 mile side trip off the main trail, so I stashed my pack in a\nbush and headed up.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI made it almost to the cliff overlook when I came around a bend and found a\nsingle bison sitting directly on the trail.\nNo way around him. He wasn’t moving.\nThat was as far as I got.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpicture\u003e\u003csource type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-on-trail-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-on-trail-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-on-trail-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-on-trail.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003cimg class=\"photo lightbox-img\" src=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-on-trail.webp\" alt=\"Bison blocking the trail to the cliff\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1600\" height=\"1067\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-on-trail-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-on-trail-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-on-trail-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/bison-on-trail.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003c/picture\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI turned around, hiked back down, grabbed my pack, and headed home.\nAll in all, it was an amazing trip — stunning scenery, some genuinely tense\nwildlife encounters, and exactly the kind of solitude I needed.\nI would absolutely do it again.\nBut next time, one lesson learned: if you’re going to bring a Garmin, keep it on\nyour person.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHere’s an album of some more images from the trip:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"album\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Image album\" data-album-id=\"album-b-0\"\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"album-frame\" style=\"aspect-ratio:1600/1067\"\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"album-scroller\"\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"album-item\" data-index=\"0\"\u003e\u003cpicture\u003e\u003csource type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/approach-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/approach-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/approach-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/approach.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003cimg class=\"album-img lightbox-img\" src=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/approach.webp\" alt=\"The approach trail\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1600\" height=\"1067\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/approach-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/approach-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/approach-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/approach.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003c/picture\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"album-item\" data-index=\"1\"\u003e\u003cpicture\u003e\u003csource type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/camp-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/camp-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/camp-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/camp.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003cimg class=\"album-img lightbox-img\" src=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/camp.webp\" alt=\"My campsite\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1600\" height=\"1067\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/camp-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/camp-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/camp-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/camp.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003c/picture\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"album-item\" data-index=\"2\"\u003e\u003cpicture\u003e\u003csource type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/sunset-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/sunset-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/sunset-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/sunset.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003cimg class=\"album-img lightbox-img\" src=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/sunset.webp\" alt=\"Sunset over the island\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1600\" height=\"1067\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/sunset-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/sunset-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/sunset-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/sunset.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003c/picture\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"album-item\" data-index=\"3\"\u003e\u003cpicture\u003e\u003csource type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/fog-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/fog-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/fog-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/fog.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003cimg class=\"album-img lightbox-img\" src=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/fog.webp\" alt=\"Morning fog\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1600\" height=\"1067\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/fog-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/fog-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/fog-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/fog.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003c/picture\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"album-item\" data-index=\"4\"\u003e\u003cpicture\u003e\u003csource type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/cliff-approach-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/cliff-approach-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/cliff-approach-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/cliff-approach.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 75vw\"/\u003e\u003cimg class=\"album-img lightbox-img\" src=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/cliff-approach.webp\" alt=\"Approaching the cliff overlook\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1600\" height=\"1067\" srcset=\"/static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/cliff-approach-sm.webp 480w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/cliff-approach-md.webp 640w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip/cliff-approach-lg.webp 1000w, /static/images/articles/2026-04-20_my-first-solo-backpacking-trip